# Extra inventory paths the monitoring play loads alongside its own
# inventory.yml. Picked up by `just ansible monitoring` (and ansible-check)
# via the .extra-inventories hook in the Justfile.
#
# Why: monitoring scrapes node_exporter on the PVE cluster + pbs01, and
# pbs-exporter's PBS_ENDPOINT also resolves a hostname. Loading these
# inventories at play time means the role can read pve_lan_ip /
# pbs_lan_ip from hostvars to build /etc/hosts entries — IPs stay
# single-sourced in the host-level inventories, no duplication into
# vms/monitoring/ansible/inventory.yml.
#
# Format: one repo-relative path per line; shell glob patterns are allowed.
# Lines starting with # are ignored. The Justfile loader glob-expands each
# line and skips anything that doesn't resolve to a real file, so guest
# inventories not yet hydrated via `just inventory <role>` simply don't
# contribute /etc/hosts entries — they show up on the next monitoring re-deploy.
pve-hosts/ansible/inventory.yml
pbs-hosts/ansible/inventory.yml
# Guest VM inventories — load ALL of them so each monitored guest's IP flows
# into /etc/hosts (via hosts_file.yml) and node_exporter scrapes resolve by
# hostname. This is a glob, not a hand-maintained list: WHICH guests get
# scraped is decided by the presence of vms/<role>/monitoring-target.yml
# (see discover_targets.yml), not by this file. Loading an unmonitored role's
# inventory is harmless — it just won't appear in the scrape list. rootca has
# no monitoring-target.yml, so loading its inventory here adds nothing.
vms/*/ansible/inventory.yml
